To implement a thread-safe singleton pattern in Java, you can use the 'Double-Checked Locking' approach. Here's a code example:

java
public class Singleton {
    private static volatile Singleton instance;

    private Singleton() {}

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}


In this implementation, the `instance` is declared as `volatile` to ensure visibility of changes across threads. The `getInstance()` method checks if the `instance` is null before acquiring a lock, and checks again inside the synchronized block to ensure only one instance is created.

